  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nThe main weather story this week will be the late season heat\nwave with strong 594-596 decameter 500 mb high over the TN \nriver valley and into central/southern IL during mid week. Will \nlikely see afternoon heat indices peak from 100-105F through \nmuch of the week with 20-40% chance of seeing heat indices peak \nabove 105F today through Friday. Highs in the mid to upper 90s \ntoday through Fri with areas from Springfield to Robinson sw \nnear 100F. Springfield already reached 97F yesterday and only 2 \ndegrees from the record high of 99F. We posted record highs in \nthe climate section below for today through Thu. We continued \nthe heat advisory across CWA today through Thu afternoon, but \nmay need to be extended it into Friday or even Saturday if \nconvection holds off and have less cloud cover. \n\nA frontal boundary meandering over parts of Iowa and WI into \nThu to keep IL in the warm sector with hot humid tropical air \nmass staying in place. Convection chances appear to stay north \nof I-80 through Wed night, then have 20-40% pops over northern \nIL just north of CWA on Thu as a short wave trof passes by to \nour north. We have slight chance of convection Friday into \nSaturday, mainly over ne CWA and eastern IL. A shortwave \ntrough move eastward across the upper MS river valley and into \nthe Great Lakes region Wednesday night and Thursday, though the \nbrunt of its convection appears to pass north of central IL. A \nstronger short wave moves se across the Midwest and IL during \nthis holiday weekend as strong mid/upper level ridge retrogrades\nback into the Great Plains, putting IL in a NW upper level flow\nregime. A backdoor cold front could slip through central IL by \nSunday and may bring 20-30% chance of convection from Friday \nthrough Sunday morning (highest pops Sat night). Hot \ntemperatures in the 90s expected through Saturday, to cool a bit\ninto the upper 80s/lower 90s early next week though still a \ngood 10 degrees warmer than normal. \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for Sep\n8-14th 50-55% chance of above normal temperatures over\ncentral/southeast IL with precipitation trending near normal.",
